  "statement": "Safetyist correctly identifies that vertical ties across chips, cloud servers, and base models create a hidden workspace that blocks institutional safety checks, requiring structural separation under antitrust law. The Safetyist position holds that consolidating semiconductor manufacturing, cloud clusters, and frontier foundational models inside a single balance sheet builds an opaque regulatory black box that evades public oversight. That diagnosis of corporate opacity is entirely accurate, yet the proposed remedy remains trapped inside the exact market framework it seeks to correct. Antitrust regulators cannot police a trillion-dollar infrastructure monopoly by appointing ethics boards or carving out compliance exceptions inside the same corporate shell. \n\nHardware-level centralization of semiconductor supply chains and proprietary accelerators functions as an incumbent capture mechanism that chokes off competitive market entry. Firms like NVIDIA and Microsoft control the physical silicon fabrication lines, the proprietary tensor processing units, and the exclusive cloud cluster access required to train frontier models. This is not the organic synergy of free markets, but an engineered vertical tollbooth designed to extract monopoly rents from every layer of the digital economy. When a single corporate entity owns the chip design, the server rack, and the model weights, no independent competitor can lease compute without paying tribute to its own market rival. \n\nFragmented regulatory silos fail to police cross-domain market power, allowing consolidated firms to shield their operations inside vertically integrated black boxes. The Federal Trade Commission and the Department of Justice must abandon piecemeal enforcement and apply the structural precedents of the 1982 AT&T breakup to physical AI infrastructure. Splitting semiconductor fabrication and cloud compute from foundational model development is the only enforcement mechanism capable of res